 ],
 "literal": "If I think, my heart burns indeed; without thinking, / if we turn it back, it is indeed not within our limit, causing pain, / touching the sky is of such nature, love; / a noble person he is not, the one whom I associated with.",
 "translation": "If I think of him, my heart burns. If I try not to think of him and turn my mind back, it is beyond my power, and it only tortures me. My love is as vast as the sky it touches. He is indeed not a noble man, the one with whom I united.",
 "modern": "அவரை நினைத்தால் என் நெஞ்சம் எரிகிறது. நினைக்காமல் மனதைத் திருப்ப முயன்றால், அது என் கட்டுப்பாட்டுக்குள் இல்லை; அது என்னை வருத்துகிறது. என் காதல் வானத்தைத் தொடும் அளவுக்குப் பெரியது. நான் நெருங்கிப் பழகிய அவர் சான்றோர் அல்லர்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her close friend about her uncontrollable grief during separation. She explains that she is caught in a painful dilemma: thinking of her lover burns her heart, but trying to turn her mind away from him is impossible as it exceeds her mental strength and only causes further distress. She describes her love as something so immense that it touches the sky. She laments that the man she intimately associated with is not a noble person, because he has left her to suffer in this manner. This poem belongs to the Neytal tiṇai (seashore landscape), which traditionally represents the themes of waiting, anxiety, and lamentation in love.",
